== The Saleebaan Dynasty ==

=== Rise ===
The unification of the [[Republic of Mazar]] and the [[Kingdom of Iskhal]] into the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] in 403 AD under [[Bugad the Great]] created a coastal power larger and wealthier than any previous polity on the peninsula. The [[Warfaa Period|Liibaan Dynasty]], which had governed the diminished Ufyen state, was unable to respond. '''[[Dhegdheer]]''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: "long-eared", i.e. watchful), commander of a strategically important ''[[Kaas|kaas]]'' in the [[Kushish]] highlands, displaced the Liibaan and assumed the kingship.

=== Reign ===
The Saleebaan governed a kingdom reduced to the [[Lake Haro]] basin, the [[Kushish]] highlands, and the Upper [[Buran River]] valley. They maintained the ''kaas'' garrison network and the [[Jowhar]] customs post, continuing to levy tariffs on goods passing between the highlands and the coast. The Saleebaan also maintained the [[Waaq]] state cult and the coronation tradition at [[Buur Waaq]], presenting themselves as the legitimate successors of the [[Geedi Period|Geedi]] founding tradition.

== The Rooble Dynasty ==

The '''Rooble Dynasty''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: ''Rooble'', "rain-bringer") governed from approximately 550 to 700 AD. The Rooble shifted the kingdom's focus toward agricultural development, expanding the Lake Haro irrigation system and investing in the terraced farming of the Kushish highlands. This period represented a relative stabilisation of the Ufyen state, though it remained the smallest of the three major southern powers (alongside the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] and the [[Kingdom of Sago]]).

== The Samatar Dynasty ==

The '''Samatar Dynasty''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: ''Samatar'', "companion in hardship") governed from approximately 700 to 800 AD. The Samatar period saw a resumption of territorial decline. The [[Kingdom of Burawa]], having consolidated its coastal holdings, began to encroach on the outer ''kaas'' forts of the Buran corridor. The Samatar kings lost control of the lower and middle Buran valley garrisons, confining the kingdom to its Lake Haro and Kushish core.

== The Guri Dynasty ==

The '''Guri Dynasty''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: ''Guri'', "home/house") was the ninth and final dynasty of the Kingdom of Ufyen, governing from approximately 800 to 900 AD. '''[[Hayaan]]''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: "fortune"), the last king of Ufyen, fell during the [[Northern Conquest of Burawa]] (893–925 AD). The [[Kingdom of Burawa]] advanced up the remaining river valleys, capturing the ''kaas'' garrisons in succession. [[Badalga-Jir]] fell, and the Ufyen highlands were incorporated into the Burawi state. The conquest ended nearly two millennia of independent highland rule. The iron mines, the ''kaas'' network, and the [[Buur Waaq]] shrine all passed into Burawi control.

